course: dictionary
lesson: 14573
title: Many meanings of pinning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Pinning comes from pin, a thin piece of metal with a sharp point. Pinning means fastening something with a pin. «Mia is pinning her science poster on the noticeboard.» The noticeboard is the clue, so pinning means fixing something in place with pins.
explain: One word can have several meanings, so always check the words around it. «The big boy was pinning his friend to the ground during the wrestling match.» The words ground and wrestling tell you that pinning means holding someone so they cannot move.
explain: Pinning can also mean blaming someone. «Tom pinned the broken vase on his little sister, but it was not her fault.» The clue is not her fault. Pinning the blame means saying someone did something wrong, often unfairly.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If fastening, holding or blaming still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Pinning means fastening something with a pin, such as a badge or a poster.
fact: Pinning someone down means holding them so they cannot move.
fact: Pinning the blame on someone means saying they did something wrong, often unfairly.
fact: On a computer, pinning means fixing a message or app at the top of the screen.
fact: «Pinning» is the -ing form of «pin». Other forms are pin, pins and pinned.
exercise: «Mia is pinning her poster on the noticeboard.» What does pinning mean here? | *Fastening something with a pin | Blaming someone unfairly | Holding someone so they cannot move | Typing a message on a screen
exercise: «The referee saw him pinning his opponent to the mat.» What does pinning mean here? | Fixing a message at the top of the screen | *Holding someone so they cannot move | Saying someone did something wrong | Fastening a badge to a shirt
exercise: Ben says, «I did not break it, so stop pinning the blame on me.» What does pinning the blame mean? | Giving someone a present | Asking someone to say sorry | Fastening the blame with a pin | *Saying someone did something wrong, often unfairly
test: «She pinned a bright badge to her jacket.» What does pinned mean here? | Held someone down | Blamed someone | Hid under a blanket | *Fastened with a pin
test: «The teacher is pinning the class rules to the wall.» What does pinning mean here? | *Fastening something with a pin | Pressing keys on a keyboard | Holding someone so they cannot move | Blaming someone unfairly
exam: On my tablet, my mum is pinning the school app to the top of the screen. What does pinning mean? | Holding the screen still so it cannot move | *Fixing an app in place at the top of the screen | Fastening a badge to a bag | Blaming the app for a mistake
